WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:03.540
professor Dave here I'm gonna tell you
about elemental analysis

00:00:07.970 --> 00:00:11.849
et's say we have an unknown
hydrocarbon

00:00:11.849 --> 00:00:14.860
that's a molecule consisting of just
hydrogen and carbon

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:19.140
let's also say we want to know the
empirical formula of the hydrocarbon

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:23.220
or the lowest whole number ratio of
carbon to hydrogen atoms

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:26.439
in the molecule. is it 1 to 2, 1 to 3

00:00:26.439 --> 00:00:31.150
3 to 5? a while back we figured out a clever way to decipher this ratio

00:00:31.150 --> 00:00:35.910
when we combust hydrocarbons, that is
burn them in the presence of oxygen

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:39.329
we get carbon dioxide and water as
products

00:00:39.329 --> 00:00:45.020
so all of the carbon in the hydrocarbon
ends up in carbon dioxide molecules

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:49.630
and all of the hydrogen ends up in water
molecules

00:00:49.630 --> 00:00:53.880
we developed this nifty apparatus
whereby a sample of hydrocarbon with known

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:54.450
mass

00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:59.079
is placed here over the flame. the tube
is open to allow oxygen to enter

00:00:59.079 --> 00:01:02.879
and then the vaporized products travel
this way through these materials

00:01:02.879 --> 00:01:06.270
one material absorbs water the other
absorbs

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:11.110
carbon dioxide. we weigh these materials
before and after the experiment

00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:14.830
and the difference will be the mass of
water and carbon dioxide

00:01:14.830 --> 00:01:17.240
absorbed respectively

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:20.710
so let's say we have a sample the benzene
but we have no idea what the ratio of

00:01:20.710 --> 00:01:22.040
carbon to hydrogen is

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:25.140
we burn a 0.342 gram sample

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:28.810
and we collect 1.156 grams of CO2

00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:32.950
in here. since CO2 weighs 44 atomic mass units

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:36.580
and a carbon atom weighs 12, each
molecule of CO2

00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:41.250
is 27.3% carbon by mass

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:44.950
that means in the CO2 there are 0.316 grams

00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:48.380
of carbon. so of the original sample
hydrocarbon

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:51.790
this much of it must have been carbon
and the remainder

00:01:51.790 --> 00:01:54.430
must have been hydrogen

00:01:54.430 --> 00:01:57.860
that makes the hydrocarbon 92.4% carbon

00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:02.009
and 7.6% hydrogen by mass

00:02:02.009 --> 00:02:06.259
we call that the percent composition of
benzene. let's go a step further and find

00:02:06.259 --> 00:02:08.700
the empirical formula

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:11.400
if we pretend we have 100 grams of the
hydrocarbon

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:15.890
92.4 grams would be carbon and 7.6
grams would be hydrogen

00:02:15.890 --> 00:02:20.560
using their atomic masses to convert to
moles we get roughly 7.6 moles

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:21.120
of each

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:24.980
which indicates a one to one ratio.
therefore

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:29.630
the empirical formula of benzene is CH. this
means that there's the same number of

00:02:29.630 --> 00:02:30.410
carbon atoms

00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:33.030
as hydrogen atoms in the molecule

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:36.400
if we want to find the molecular formula
which is the actual number of

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:40.220
atoms of each element in a molecule, we need
the molecular mass

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:44.000
let's say we know that for benzene the
molecular mass is 78

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:47.340
one unit of the empirical formula weighs
13

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:52.210
and 78 divided by 13 is six, this means
we need six units

00:02:52.210 --> 00:02:57.090
the empirical formula to get the
molecular formula or C6H6

00:02:57.520 --> 00:02:58.520
let's check comprehension

